.TH GARRETT 8 "November 2026" "garrett" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
garrett \- host intrusion sentinel that stops what it catches
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B garrett enroll
.B \-\-server
.I address
.RB [ \-\-key
.IR key ]
.RB [ \-\-mode
.IR kill | decide | pause | report ]
.RB [ \-\-label
.IR name ]
.br
.B garrett status
.br
.B garrett forget
.br
.B garrett cases
.br
.B garrett trust\fR|\fBstop\fR|\fBrelease\fR|\fBrestore
.I case
.br
.B garrett confirm
.I case
.br
.B garrett learned
.RB [ \-\-reset ]
.br
.B garrett shield
.RB [ on | off | allow
.IR name | \fBcut\fR
.IR name ]
.br
.B garrett traffic
.br
.B garrett hardware
.br
.B garrett update
.RB [ \-\-check ]
.br
.B garrett run
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B garrett
watches this machine's running programs and their connections, scores them on
the machine, and pauses or ends a program it judges hostile. Installed
with nothing else, it protects the machine it is on and keeps its cases there.
Enrolled, it reports cases to a Sentinel service that you run, and applies what
that service has learned from the rest of your machines.
.PP
It normally runs as the
.B garrett
systemd service. Enrolling starts the service.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B enroll
Enrol this machine with a Sentinel service and start the service. The
enrolment key may be given in the environment variable
.B SENTINEL_ENROLL_KEY
instead of on the command line.
.TP
.B status
Show which service this machine reports to, and its response mode.
.TP
.B forget
Stop the service and remove this machine's credential.
.TP
.B cases
List the latest cases on this machine.
.TP
.BI trust " case"
This program is mine: leave it alone from now on.
.TP
.BI stop " case" "\fR, \fP" release " case"
Decide a program the agent paused: end it, or let it continue.
.TP
.BI restore " case"
Put back everything that was cleaned up after a stop: quarantined files and
switched-off startup entries.
.TP
.BI confirm " case"
That was an attack. If the program runs again the agent will not leave it
alone, and the verdict is something it learns from.
.TP
.B learned
What this machine has learned from your verdicts, and whether it is still
settling in.
.B \-\-reset
forgets it. See
.BR LEARNING .
.TP
.B shield
The privacy shield: which trackers are refused at the firewall, which are left
open and why.
.B on
and
.B off
switch it on this machine.
.BI allow " name"
never refuses a tracker;
.BI cut " name"
refuses it although a program here uses it. See
.BR PRIVACY .
.TP
.B traffic
Which programs on this machine were seen talking to which trackers.
.TP
.B hardware
What this machine says about its firmware, its boot chain and its kernel, and
whether any of it changed since the agent last read it. See
.BR "BELOW THE SYSTEM" .
.TP
.B update
Install the newest version now.
.B \-\-check
only says whether there is one. The running agent does this by itself a few
times a day; see
.BR UPDATES .
.TP
.B run
Watch and report in the foreground. The service runs this.
.SH RESPONSE MODES
.TP
.B kill
End a hostile program and everything it started, at once. The default.
.TP
.B decide
Freeze it, then let the service's decision model say stop, release or hold.
.TP
.B pause
Freeze it and wait for an operator.
.TP
.B report
Only report.
.PP
The service may hold a machine to a lower mode than it asked for.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/garrett/garrett.conf
Optional, mode 0600. Lines
.BR SERVER= ,
.BR ENROLL_KEY= ,
and optionally
.BR MODE= ,
.BR LABEL= ,
.BR CLEAN=off ,
.BR UPDATE=off ,
.B SHIELD=off
and
.BR LEARN_HOURS= .
When this file exists and the machine is not enrolled, the service enrols
itself on first start.
.TP
.I /var/lib/garrett/
This machine's credential and what the agent remembers about it.
.SH LEARNING
A fresh install settles in for a day
.RB ( LEARN_HOURS ).
What is plainly an attack, such as a web server starting a shell that
downloads and runs a script, is stopped from the first second. A program that
only its score speaks against is reported and left running, during that day
and after it, until you say what it is with
.B trust
or
.BR confirm :
it was on the machine before garrett knew the machine, and may be yours.
.PP
On a machine with no service, every verdict you give
.RB ( trust ,
.BR restore ,
.BR release ,
.BR stop ,
.BR confirm )
is kept as the case's signals and nothing else. A signal you keep calling
yours comes to count for less here, one you keep confirming for more, by at
most two points, and never so far that a confirmed attack or a known attack
pattern would go unreported. Two signals that your confirmed attacks share
become a sign of attack on this machine. No model is involved. An enrolled
machine learns from its service instead.
.SH PRIVACY
The privacy shield is on unless you switch it off. It changes no setting of
the operating system. It looks up the names advertising and tracking networks
are reached by and notes which program on this machine talks to them; that
record stays on the machine.
.PP
After the settling-in day it refuses a tracker at the firewall when the
tracker answers from a network of its own company, so that nothing else is
behind the address, and no program other than a browser was seen using it
here. A tracker that your own software uses is left open and named. A tracker
that shares its addresses with sites people visit is measured and never
refused, because refusing the address would break those sites. A browser can
still reach a refused tracker through a proxy or a VPN.
.SH BELOW THE SYSTEM
An attack on the firmware or the boot chain survives a reinstall and is out of
reach of anything that only watches programs. garrett cannot stand inside the
firmware and never writes to it. It reads what the machine says about itself
down there, every few minutes, remembers it, and reports a change that an
update does not explain:
.IP \(bu 2
Secure Boot (on a Mac, System Integrity Protection) was on and is now off.
.IP \(bu 2
The firmware's list of what to start has a new or changed entry, which is
where a boot kit puts itself. The entry a firmware update makes for itself is
not reported.
.IP \(bu 2
The code the machine starts from changed: the boot sector of a disk, the space
behind it where GRUB keeps its core on a BIOS machine, or a boot loader file on
the firmware's partition, and no boot loader update was installed that would
explain it. These are read once an hour.
.IP \(bu 2
The firmware is older than it was.
.IP \(bu 2
The security chip's measurement of the firmware changed while the firmware
still gives the same version and date: its code was altered without an update.
.IP \(bu 2
On Linux, a kernel module is loaded that is not among the modules installed
for the running kernel.
.IP \(bu 2
Windows was restarted with its driver signature checks off.
.PP
The first reading is the starting point and nothing is reported from it. A
machine that does not tell one of these things (a virtual machine without a
security chip, an old board) is simply not judged on it;
.B garrett hardware
shows what could be read. Nothing is ever changed or undone down there: each
report is a case for you to judge.
.SH AT THE NETWORK CARD
Things happen on the wire, and at the sockets of the machine, that no program
on it starts:
.IP \(bu 2
Another machine on the network starts answering in place of the gateway, so
that what this machine sends out passes through it. garrett reads the system's
table of neighbours every two seconds (every thirty on a Mac, and every thirty
for IPv6 everywhere) and reports the moment the gateway's hardware address
becomes that of a machine that was already on the network, or starts going
back and forth between two, which is what happens while the true gateway and
an impostor both keep answering. A new router is learned without a word.
.IP \(bu 2
On Linux and macOS, a machine on the network tries port after port here.
garrett reads the first packet of each arriving connection attempt at the
network card: who sent it and which port it asked for, never what follows.
Thirty different ports from one neighbour in a minute are reported, once an
hour for each neighbour. Scans from the open internet are not reported.
.IP \(bu 2
On Linux and Windows, a keyboard this machine has never had is plugged in and
a command window opens within seconds: how a device that types by itself
works. Only the names of the keyboards are read, never a key.
.PP
All of these are reported and nothing is done about them.
.SH UPDATES
The running agent asks the project's site a few times a day whether a newer
version is out, and installs it. It installs nothing unless the site's list of
program files carries the project's signature, names a version newer than the
one installed, and matches the file it downloaded. Where this package is
installed, apt does the installing from the project's signed repository.
.B UPDATE=off
in the configuration file turns this off.
.SH FIREWALL
When the service has fleet blocking on, garrett keeps one nftables table named
.BR garrett .
It is removed when the service stops and when the package is removed.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR nft (8)
